        highestBid = price;
        highestBidder = msg.sender;
    }

    function endAuction() public {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Only the owner can end the auction.");
        require(block.timestamp >= auctionEnd, "Auction hasn't ended yet.");

        emit AuctionEnded(highestBid, highestBidder);
        selfdestruct(highestBidder);
    }

    event AuctionEnded(uint highestBid, address highestBidder);
}
```

This smart contract allows users to bid on an item in a decentralized auction. The contract keeps track of the highest bid, the bidder, and the auction end time. Users can place bids, and the contract ensures that each bid is higher than the current highest bid and that the auction has not ended. The contract also allows the owner to end the auction and transfer the funds to the highest bidder.

This is a simple example, and a real-world complex smart contract might include additional features, such as a minimum bid amount, a reserve price, or a mechanism for the seller to accept or reject bids. It's important to thoroughly test and audit any smart contract before deploying it to a live blockchain.

